//? Title: Noncentral distribution tails for power and inference
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Find a rejection cutoff and solve for power

In an upper-tail test, the central law sets the rejection cutoff.
The noncentral law gives the probability of exceeding that cutoff under
an alternative. The inverse calculation finds the noncentrality that
would give a target power at the same cutoff. Mapping that parameter
to an effect size depends on the study design.

```{cpp, id=noncentral-power-and-cutoffs, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/random/noncentral_distributions.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::random;
    constexpr double alpha = .05, target = .8;
    const double chi_cutoff = noncentral_chi_square_quantile(alpha, 4, 0, false);
    const double f_cutoff = noncentral_f_quantile(alpha, 4, 20, 0, false);
    const double chi_power = noncentral_chi_square_cdf(chi_cutoff, 4, 10, false);
    const double f_power = noncentral_f_cdf(f_cutoff, 4, 20, 10, false);
    const double chi_target = noncentral_chi_square_noncentrality(target, chi_cutoff, 4, false);
    const double f_target = noncentral_f_noncentrality(target, f_cutoff, 4, 20, false);
    std::println("| Law | 5% cutoff | Power at noncentrality 10 | Noncentrality for 80% power |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| Chi-square, 4 df | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", chi_cutoff, chi_power, chi_target);
    std::println("| F, 4 and 20 df | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", f_cutoff, f_power, f_target);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Law | 5% cutoff | Power at noncentrality 10 | Noncentrality for 80% power |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Chi-square, 4 df | 9.488 | 0.716 | 11.935 |
| F, 4 and 20 df | 2.866 | 0.601 | 15.094 |
// result_end

> Invert a noncentral t reference

For 20 observations, the one-sample t statistic has 19 degrees of freedom.
Its noncentrality equals the population standardized mean difference
times the square root of 20. The program computes upper-tail power at
a standardized difference of 0.5. For an observed t statistic of 2.5,
it then finds the noncentralities where the observed value lies in each
2.5% tail and converts those endpoints to the original effect scale.

```{cpp, id=noncentral-t-power-and-interval, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/random/noncentral_distributions.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::random;
    constexpr double n = 20, df = n - 1, observed_t = 2.5;
    const auto root_n = std::sqrt(n);
    const auto cutoff = noncentral_t_quantile(.05, df, 0, false);
    const auto power = noncentral_t_cdf(cutoff, df, .5 * root_n, false);
    const auto lower = noncentral_t_noncentrality(.025, observed_t, df, false) / root_n;
    const auto upper = noncentral_t_noncentrality(.025, observed_t, df, true) / root_n;
    std::println("The 5% upper-tail cutoff is {:.3f}, giving power {:.3f} at a standardized difference of 0.5.",
                 cutoff, power);
    std::println("");
    std::println("For t = 2.5, the 95% interval on the standardized difference is [{:.3f}, {:.3f}].",
                 lower, upper);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
The 5% upper-tail cutoff is 1.729, giving power 0.695 at a standardized difference of 0.5.

For t = 2.5, the 95% interval on the standardized difference is [0.080, 1.025].
// result_end

